ZERO FLASH: FIRMWARE EDITION
Version 0.14; original Zero implementation.

This program runs under UEFI BEFORE Zero OS starts. It uses the firmware's
Simple File System and Block I/O interfaces. It is not an in-kernel USB
mass-storage driver and cannot run after ExitBootServices. BIOS-only machines
can boot the written Zero image but cannot run this firmware writer.

Files
FLASHX64.EFI is the x86-64 program.
FLASHIA32.EFI is the IA32 program; the boot volume uses the short alias
EFI/BOOT/FLASH32.EFI. The Zero boot menu launches the matching edition.
Standalone firmware launch is also supported.
All program, composition, hashing and container-validation source is Zero.

Source selection
Place system.zmedia at the ROOT of a firmware-readable volume. A separate
FAT-formatted source volume is normally needed because the small Zero ESP
cannot hold a complete system image. The program asks existing firmware
drivers to connect their devices, then scans up to1024 firmware handles.
It lists up to32 matching source volumes with their image size and complete
firmware device path. One source is selected automatically; multiple sources
require a visible number starting at1. Escape cancels.
The whole selected image is loaded and its exact container length, reserved
fields and SHA256 are verified BEFORE any target is written. No source bytes
are reread during writing. Images are bounded to256MiB payload; adequate free
firmware memory is required. SHA256 checks integrity, not publisher identity.

Target selection
Only present, writable, removable, whole-device USB Block I/O handles with
512-byte sectors and valid alignment up to4096 bytes are eligible. Internal
fixed disks, partitions, read-only media and targets too small for the payload
are excluded. Both the program's boot disk and the selected image's source
disk are excluded using their complete firmware device-path ancestry.

Each eligible target is listed with number, capacity, media ID and full
firmware path. The user selects a target number and types ERASE. The program
then reacquires its protocols and compares the copied device path, media ID,
capacity, removable/media flags, sector size and alignment before writing.
A changed or removed device fails before the first write.

Writing and verification
The container header is not written to the target: its validated disk payload
is written in64KiB aligned chunks. If the disk is larger, the final up to33
sectors outside the payload are cleared to remove a stale backup GPT. The
middle area outside the payload and tail is preserved; this is not a secure
erasure tool. The program flushes firmware block I/O and rereads every written
payload byte and cleared tail byte before reporting success.
Any failed write, flush or readback is reported as failure. A partial target
may no longer boot; no rollback is claimed. Escape and incorrect confirmation
before writing preserve the target.

Platform details
IA32 allocations use AllocateMaxAddress with a4GiB address ceiling. Every
64-bit block address is passed as distinct low and high ABI words, including
tail cleanup on disks larger than2TiB. Both editions use their own aligned
coroutine stacks and explicit firmware page allocations.

Build
Compile media/firmware-compose.zero with the Zero native compiler. Invoke it
with PROJECT NEW_SOURCE, or PROJECT NEW_SOURCE --ia32. Existing output paths
are refused. The composer checks that every required ABI replacement matches
and that the source-discovery insertion occurs exactly once. Compile the
result with Zero --efi or --efi32 respectively. Canonical composed sources are
media/firmware.zero and media/firmware32.zero.

Current limits
Only UEFI x86-64 and IA32; removable USB recognized by firmware;512-byte disk
sectors; up to32 source volumes and32 targets; max256MiB payload. Firmware
support determines which USB controllers and source filesystems are usable.
There is no native Zero kernel USB stack, no BIOS writer edition, no signature
trust store and no guarantee of power-loss recovery during a write.
